        <!-- ═══ WHICH UNIT? ═══ -->
        <h2 style="font-family: 'Georgia', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary-dark); margin: 40px 0 16px;">Which Unit Should I Rent?</h2>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 18px;">Find the line that sounds like you:</p>

        <ul style="margin: 0 0 20px; padding-left: 22px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>A 1–2 bedroom apartment, or you're between homes.</strong> Take the <a href="/20ft-storage-container-rental" style="color: var(--primary-dark); font-weight: 600;">20ft at $130/mo</a>. ~160 sq ft is enough for the furniture, appliances, and boxes without paying for space you won't fill.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>A 3–4 bedroom house, or a house plus a garage.</strong> Take the <a href="/40ft-storage-container-rental" style="color: var(--primary-dark); font-weight: 600;">40ft at $230/mo</a>. Splitting a household across two 20ft units costs more and makes everything harder to reach.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Tall items, shelving, or you want to work inside the unit.</strong> Ask for the <a href="/40ft-storage-container-rental" style="color: var(--primary-dark); font-weight: 600;">40ft High Cube</a> — 9.5 ft of clearance instead of 8.5 ft.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Tools, materials, and a truck that needs to pull up to the door.</strong> Either container works; the 20ft is usually enough for a <a href="/construction-equipment-storage" style="color: var(--primary-dark); font-weight: 600;">crew's tools and materials</a>, the 40ft if you're storing equipment or scaffolding. Both are drive-up with wide driveways for flatbeds and trailers.</li>
